Native Linux x86_64 bootstrap installation

The toolchain holds GCC and G++ 13.5.0, binutils 2.41, musl 1.2.5 and the
static C and C++ runtimes. The userland holds Bash 5.2.15, Make 4.2.1,
coreutils 6.10, findutils 4.2.33, diffutils 2.7, sed 4.0.9, grep 2.4,
gawk 3.0.4, tar 1.12, gzip 1.2.4, bzip2 1.0.8, patch 2.5.9, m4 1.4.7,
Flex 2.6.4 and Bison 3.4.1. An installation holds the toolchain, the
userland or both.

Keep the installation directory together when copying or moving it, and add
its bin directory to PATH. The launchers in bin find their programs, headers,
libraries and data relative to the installation through Linux /proc/self/exe,
so no absolute path is compiled into them.

    gcc hello.c -o hello
    g++ -std=gnu++20 -pthread hello.cc -o hello
    make -j4

Programs compile and link statically by default. The compiler targets native
x86_64 only, without shared executables, multilib or languages other than C
and C++. C++ support is that of GCC 13.5.0, which defaults to C++17 and
provides C++20 facilities such as std::format, ranges and the time zone
database, whose data is built into libstdc++. -nostdinc and -nostdinc++ turn
off the installed default include directories.

The compiler ignores inherited compiler, include and library search
variables, so pass project dependencies with -I, -L and other arguments.
Launchers put this installation's bin in front of PATH and select the
installed Bash as the shell. Bison, Flex, gawk, locate and updatedb also get
the installed m4, Bison data, awk library and locate database, and other
programs keep the caller's values of those settings. An unset TMPDIR becomes
/tmp, and a set one is kept. Makefile and command-line SHELL settings take
precedence over the installed default shell.

updatedb and locate use var/locatedb inside the installation by default.
updatedb --output and locate --database select another database, and the
usual updatedb traversal options apply.

share/bootstrap/manifest.tsv lists every installed path with the Buck label
it comes from. share/licenses holds the upstream licenses and the launcher's
notice.
